findings.body:
**Credentialed CORS now allows any origin.** Removing the allowlist and returning `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: origin ?? "*"` together with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` means any malicious website can trigger credentialed cross-origin requests (cookies / auth headers) and read the responses.

Failure mode: an attacker page at `https://evil.example` sends a credentialed `fetch` to this API; the response includes `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://evil.example` and `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, so the browser exposes the body to the attacker script.

Native mobile clients are not gated by browser CORS — they do not need origin reflection. Restore an explicit allowlist (web app origins + any intentional mobile-webview origins), and never pair `*` with credentials:

```suggestion
const ALLOWED = new Set(["https://app.example.com"]);

export function corsHeaders(origin: string | null): Record<string, string> {
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return {};
}
```
